# SQL is applied verbatim, and a function body between $$ ... $$ keeps its
# line endings inside pg_proc.prosrc. Two consequences, both learned the hard
# way on 21 Sep 2026 when scripts/migration-harness.sh caught a DOWN chain that
# could not restore what the migrations created:
#
#   1. A file saved with CRLF stores a DIFFERENT function body from the same
#      file saved with LF. Same code, different checksum. A DOWN file written
#      on Windows cannot restore byte-for-byte what a migration written with
#      LF created.
#
#   2. Therefore git must never rewrite the line endings of a .sql file. Some
#      migrations in this repository are CRLF and are ALREADY APPLIED to the
#      linked project: production's stored bodies carry those line endings,
#      and a rebuild from this repository has to reproduce them exactly.
#      Normalising them here would not remove drift, it would create it.
#
# So: no conversion, in either direction. What is committed is what is
# applied. New SQL should be written with LF — that is a convention for the
# author, not something git may impose on files that already exist.
#
# `whitespace=-trailing-space` for the same reason. A restored body carries the
# line endings of the migration that created it, so a DOWN file can legitimately
# hold CRLF inside an otherwise-LF file. Git's whitespace checker reads that CR
# as trailing whitespace and `git diff --check` reports hundreds of them. The
# bytes are correct and the migration harness proves it; the lint is the thing
# that is wrong here, so it is switched off for SQL rather than the bytes being
# "fixed" into a DOWN that restores the wrong function.
*.sql -text whitespace=-trailing-space

# Shell scripts run inside the Linux container as well as on the host; a CR at
# the end of a shebang line is a "bad interpreter" error that reads as a
# missing file.
*.sh text eol=lf
